### What is a poison-pill message?

A poison pill is a record that a consumer can never process successfully. Every attempt fails with the same exception. Because Kafka processes records in offset order and only commits the offset after success, the consumer never advances past the poison pill, refetches it on every poll, and fails again forever. The entire partition is blocked.

```java
package com.bank.error;

public class PoisonPillScenario {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Partition 3 records:");
        System.out.println("  offset 100: valid transfer SAV2001");
        System.out.println("  offset 101: POISON PILL (invalid amount: -50000)");
        System.out.println("  offset 102: valid transfer SAV3045");
        System.out.println("  offset 103: valid transfer SAV1122");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Without DLT:");
        System.out.println("  Consumer processes offset 100 -- OK");
        System.out.println("  Consumer processes offset 101 -- FAIL");
        System.out.println("  Consumer retries offset 101  -- FAIL");
        System.out.println("  Consumer retries offset 101  -- FAIL (forever)");
        System.out.println("  Offsets 102, 103 NEVER processed!");
    }
}
```

```text
Partition 3 records:
  offset 100: valid transfer SAV2001
  offset 101: POISON PILL (invalid amount: -50000)
  offset 102: valid transfer SAV3045
  offset 103: valid transfer SAV1122

Without DLT:
  Consumer processes offset 100 -- OK
  Consumer processes offset 101 -- FAIL
  Consumer retries offset 101  -- FAIL
  Consumer retries offset 101  -- FAIL (forever)
  Offsets 102, 103 NEVER processed!
```

### How does a dead letter topic fix this?

After a bounded number of retries, the consumer publishes the failing record to a dead letter topic with error metadata, commits the offset to move past it, and continues processing the rest of the partition.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.DefaultErrorHandler;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer;
import org.springframework.util.backoff.FixedBackOff;

public class DltConfiguration {
    public static DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler(
            KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        // After 3 retries with 1-second wait, publish to DLT
        DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer recoverer =
            new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(kafkaTemplate);
        DefaultErrorHandler handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(
            recoverer, new FixedBackOff(1000L, 3));
        return handler;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("DLT flow:");
        System.out.println("  offset 101: try 1 -- FAIL");
        System.out.println("  offset 101: try 2 -- FAIL (wait 1s)");
        System.out.println("  offset 101: try 3 -- FAIL (wait 1s)");
        System.out.println("  offset 101: publish to transfers.DLT");
        System.out.println("  offset 101: commit, advance to 102");
        System.out.println("  offset 102: process -- OK");
        System.out.println("  Partition keeps flowing!");
    }
}
```

```text
DLT flow:
  offset 101: try 1 -- FAIL
  offset 101: try 2 -- FAIL (wait 1s)
  offset 101: try 3 -- FAIL (wait 1s)
  offset 101: publish to transfers.DLT
  offset 101: commit, advance to 102
  offset 102: process -- OK
  Partition keeps flowing!
```

### How do you classify exceptions?

Not all failures deserve retries. Transient failures like a database timeout may succeed on retry. Permanent failures like a validation error will never succeed. Route permanent failures straight to the DLT without wasting retries.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.kafka.listener.DefaultErrorHandler;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer;
import org.springframework.util.backoff.FixedBackOff;

public class ExceptionClassification {
    public static DefaultErrorHandler classifiedHandler(
            DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer recoverer) {
        DefaultErrorHandler handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(
            recoverer, new FixedBackOff(1000L, 3));
        // These go straight to DLT -- no retries
        handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(
            IllegalArgumentException.class,   // validation error
            com.fasterxml.jackson.core.JsonParseException.class  // bad JSON
        );
        return handler;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Exception classification:");
        System.out.println("  Transient (retry):  DatabaseTimeoutException");
        System.out.println("  Transient (retry):  SocketTimeoutException");
        System.out.println("  Permanent (DLT):    IllegalArgumentException");
        System.out.println("  Permanent (DLT):    JsonParseException");
        System.out.println("  Permanent (DLT):    SerializationException");
    }
}
```

```text
Exception classification:
  Transient (retry):  DatabaseTimeoutException
  Transient (retry):  SocketTimeoutException
  Permanent (DLT):    IllegalArgumentException
  Permanent (DLT):    JsonParseException
  Permanent (DLT):    SerializationException
```

### How do you handle deserialization poison pills?

Deserialization failures happen inside the Kafka client before your business logic runs. A safe deserializer wraps the real deserializer and produces a null value with the failure in a header instead of crashing.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.util.Properties;

public class SafeDeserializerConfig {
    public static Properties config() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092");
        props.put("group.id", "settlement-service");
        // Wrap the real deserializer
        props.put("value.deserializer",
            "org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.ErrorHandlingDeserializer");
        props.put("spring.deserializer.value.delegate.class",
            "org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonDeserializer");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Without ErrorHandlingDeserializer:");
        System.out.println("  Bad bytes -> SerializationException -> consumer stuck");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("With ErrorHandlingDeserializer:");
        System.out.println("  Bad bytes -> null value + error header -> DLT");
    }
}
```

```text
Without ErrorHandlingDeserializer:
  Bad bytes -> SerializationException -> consumer stuck

With ErrorHandlingDeserializer:
  Bad bytes -> null value + error header -> DLT
```

### What metadata should the DLT record carry?

The dead letter record must carry enough context to debug and replay.

```java
package com.bank.dlt;

public class DltMetadata {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("DLT record headers (auto-added by Spring):");
        System.out.println("  kafka_dlt-original-topic     : transfers");
        System.out.println("  kafka_dlt-original-partition  : 3");
        System.out.println("  kafka_dlt-original-offset     : 101");
        System.out.println("  kafka_dlt-original-timestamp  : 1719849600000");
        System.out.println("  kafka_dlt-exception-fqcn      : IllegalArgumentException");
        System.out.println("  kafka_dlt-exception-message   : Negative amount");
        System.out.println("  kafka_dlt-exception-stacktrace: ...");
    }
}
```

