### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class PoisonPillMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: infinite retries on a permanent failure
        System.out.println("WRONG: retries=MAX on a validation error");
        System.out.println("  Consumer retries forever, partition stuck");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: classify and bound retries
        System.out.println("CORRECT: 3 retries, then DLT, validation is not-retryable");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: DLT with no monitoring
        System.out.println("WRONG: DLT exists but nobody watches it");
        System.out.println("  Failures accumulate silently -- data loss");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: alert on DLT depth, assign ownership
        System.out.println("CORRECT: alert on transfers.DLT lag > 0");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: retries=MAX on a validation error
  Consumer retries forever, partition stuck

CORRECT: 3 retries, then DLT, validation is not-retryable

WRONG: DLT exists but nobody watches it
  Failures accumulate silently -- data loss

CORRECT: alert on transfers.DLT lag > 0
```

### How does Spring Kafka handle poison pills declaratively?

Spring Kafka provides `DefaultErrorHandler` with `DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer` that handles the retry-then-DLT flow automatically. You configure the backoff policy and the recoverer, and any message that exhausts retries is published to the DLT topic with all the failure headers attached. The `ErrorHandlingDeserializer` wrapper catches deserialization failures before they reach your listener.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class SpringKafkaDltConfig {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Spring Kafka declarative DLT handling:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  // 1. Wrap deserializer for poison deserialization");
        System.out.println("  props.put(VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,");
        System.out.println("      ErrorHandlingDeserializer.class);");
        System.out.println("  props.put(SPRING_DESERIALIZER_VALUE_DELEGATE_CLASS,");
        System.out.println("      StringDeserializer.class);");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  // 2. Configure error handler with DLT recoverer");
        System.out.println("  DefaultErrorHandler handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(");
        System.out.println("      new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(kafkaTemplate),");
        System.out.println("      new FixedBackOff(1000L, 3L)");
        System.out.println("  );");
        System.out.println("  // 3. Non-retryable exceptions skip straight to DLT");
        System.out.println("  handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(");
        System.out.println("      DeserializationException.class,");
        System.out.println("      ValidationException.class);");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Spring Kafka declarative DLT handling:

  // 1. Wrap deserializer for poison deserialization
  props.put(VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
      ErrorHandlingDeserializer.class);
  props.put(SPRING_DESERIALIZER_VALUE_DELEGATE_CLASS,
      StringDeserializer.class);

  // 2. Configure error handler with DLT recoverer
  DefaultErrorHandler handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(
      new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(kafkaTemplate),
      new FixedBackOff(1000L, 3L)
  );
  // 3. Non-retryable exceptions skip straight to DLT
  handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(
      DeserializationException.class,
      ValidationException.class);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A dead letter topic is not a fix -- it is an isolation mechanism. Without monitoring on its depth, root-cause classification, a replay procedure, and clear ownership, it quietly becomes a graveyard where failures pile up unnoticed. Consumers should be idempotent so that when you eventually replay a dead letter record, you do not create duplicate side effects.
